FreeBSD's pw(8), vendored.

Upstream:  freebsd/freebsd-src, releng/15.0
Path:      usr.sbin/pw
Revision:  1efb9810ba8caa550e0b8459117a36fde0daaec9 (2025-10-04)

WHY IT IS HERE

NextBSD's pw routes: a directory account is handled in the DirectoryServices
property lists, and a system account belongs in /etc/master.passwd. The second
half is a packaging contract -- every account a port creates is a system
account, and ports emit five fixed command forms -- so it has to behave exactly
as it always has. The surest way to behave exactly like FreeBSD's pw is to be
FreeBSD's pw.

It used to be exec'd as a second binary that compat relocated to
/usr/libexec/bsd/pw. That left one command only half working on its own,
depending on an undocumented path in another repository, and it meant the two
repositories could only be merged in an order where one of them was broken.
Vendoring removes all of that: one binary, at one path, from one tree.

WHAT WAS CHANGED

One file, and one line in it:

  pw.c -> bsd_main.c, and its main() renamed to pw_bsd_main().

The rename is in the filename so that the file we edit is the only file whose
name differs from upstream.
The entry point also gains a prototype immediately above itself, so the front
end's call is checked against the definition. That is the whole diff.
 Everything else is byte-identical, so re-syncing is
a diff and never a merge.

NIS is not configured anywhere in NextBSD, so pw_nis.c and pw_vpw.c are
unreachable; they are built only because pw_user.c and pw_group.c reference
them.

Not built on Darwin: libutil's pw_* and login_cap do not exist there. The
front end stubs pw_bsd_main() out so a host syntax check still compiles.

RE-SYNCING

  1. Fetch the new upstream usr.sbin/pw.
  2. Diff it against this directory, remembering bsd_main.c is upstream's pw.c.
  3. Apply, re-apply the main() rename, and update Revision: above.

A change to the five command forms ports use, or to how uids are allocated, is
worth reading closely -- src/accounts/pw/tests covers both.
